    </region>
    <region left="0" top="1836" width="778" height="83" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p><span style="font-weight: bold;">Conclusion:</span> For the three concepts, the LED requirement is identical because the same 120 WS2812B LEDs are used. The calculated transmission time is therefore 3.60 ms for all three concepts, which is below the 20 ms period associated with the 50 Hz lighting target. The main difference is the processing capacity available around the LED update. The Arduino Nano has approximately 320,000 clock cycles available per update period, compared with 3.00 million for the Pico 2 and 4.80 million for the ESP32.</p></content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="1935" width="760" height="23" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="760" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="text-decoration: underline;">1.9 MPU6050 I²C Data Transfer Time:</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="1962" width="86" height="29" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <math>
        <input>
          <e type="operand">N.bytes</e>
          <e type="operand">12</e>
          <e type="operator" args="2">:</e>
        </input>
      </math>
    </region>
    <region left="333" top="1971" width="430" height="20" color="#000000" fontSize="8">
      <text lang="eng" width="430" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-size: 8px;">//Six 16-bit accelerometer and gyroscope readings</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="1998" width="109" height="29" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <math>
        <input>
          <e type="operand">f.I2C</e>
          <e type="operand">400</e>
          <e type="operand" style="unit">kHz</e>
          <e type="operator" args="2">*</e>
          <e type="operator" args="2">:</e>
        </input>
      </math>
    </region>
    <region left="333" top="1998" width="439" height="20" color="#000000" fontSize="10"><text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10"><content><p style="font-size: 8px;">//I²C bus clock frequency (fast mode)</p></content></text></region><region left="0" top="2034" width="188" height="51" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <math>
        <input>
          <e type="operand">t.IMU</e>
          <e type="operand">N.bytes</e>
          <e type="operand">8</e>
          <e type="operator" args="2">*</e>
          <e type="operand">f.I2C</e>
          <e type="operator" args="2">/</e>
          <e type="operator" args="2">:</e>
        </input>
        <contract>
          <e type="operand" style="unit">ms</e>
        </contract>
        <result action="numeric">
          <e type="operand">0.24</e>
        </result>
      </math>
    </region>
    <region left="333" top="2052" width="439" height="20" color="#000000" fontSize="10"><text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10"><content><p style="font-size: 8px;">//Time to read one set of sensor data over I²C (8 bits per byte)</p></content></text></region><region left="0" top="2088" width="766" height="38" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" width="760" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p><span style="font-weight: bold;">Conclusion:</span> The sensor data transfer time is 0.24 ms, which is well below the 100 ms motion detection target. Communication overhead and software processing add to this, but leave a large margin.</p></content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="2151" width="157" height="26" color="#000000" fontSize="12">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
